## When to use has-meta-type vs instance-of

| Predicate | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `instance-of` | A is an individual of class B (e.g., a specific decision memorial is instance-of `:mm/Decision`) |
| `has-meta-type` | A's class is itself an instance of B (e.g., `:mm/Decision` is-instance-of `:dt/Class`, expressed via meta-type) |

Use `has-meta-type:` for reflective introspection — querying "what kind of thing is this thing's type?"

## Formal ontology mappings

Aligned predicates from established formal vocabularies:

- **RDF** `rdf:type` — close analogue (rdf:type is the immediate-class relation; has-meta-type applies the same shape at the meta-level via OWL Full semantics)
- **OWL** — no direct equivalent (OWL DL forbids metaclasses; OWL Full / OWL 2 RL admit them via punning)
- **RDFS** — no direct equivalent (RDFS predates metaclass treatment)
- **DC Terms** — no direct equivalent
- **PROV-O** — no direct equivalent
- **SKOS** — no direct equivalent

